\section{CONCLUSION}
In this chapter, the hull geometry of the vessel was developed using the NPL systematic series. The applicability of the NPL series was verified by comparing the required design parameters with the specified criteria for NPL and UCL. The parent hull offsets were then scaled and transformed according to the design requirements to generate the final offset table.
\vspace{\baselineskip}\par\noindent The generated offsets were used to develop the line plan and three-dimensional hull model in Rhino. The model was subsequently imported into Maxsurf and verified through hydrostatic calculations at the design waterline. Bonjean calculations and hydrostatic particulars were also determined, providing the necessary geometric and hydrostatic data for the subsequent stages of the vessel design.
\section{SCOPE FOR NEXT CHAPTER}
With the hull geometry and hydrostatic characteristics established, the next chapter focuses on the resistance of the vessel. The developed hull form will be analyzed to determine its resistance characteristics at the required operating speeds.
\vspace{\baselineskip}\par\noindent The resistance analysis will include the selection of an appropriate resistance prediction method, the calculation of the major resistance components, and the estimation of the total resistance and the effective power required for the vessel. The results will subsequently provide the basis for the calculations of the propulsion system and the power requirement.

